# minimum qore version
%requires qore >= 2.0

# don't use "$" for vars, members, and methods, assume local variable scope
%modern

%requires Mime >= 1.3
%requires(reexport) DataProvider
%requires(reexport) RestSchemaActions
%requires StripeRestClient
%requires RestClientIo

%requires json

module StripeDataProvider {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "user module providing a data provider API for Stripe";
    author = "Qore Technologies <info@qoretechnologies.com>";
    url = "https://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        # register the data provider factory
        DataProvider::registerFactory(new StripeDataProviderFactory());

        # register the data provider application
        DataProviderActionCatalog::registerApp(<DataProviderAppInfo>{
            "name": StripeDataProvider::AppName,
            "presentation_source_locale": "en",
            "display_name": "Stripe",
            "short_desc": "Take payments and manage customers, invoices and payouts in Stripe",
            "desc": "Provides access to [Stripe](https://stripe.com), the payments platform, for taking "
                "money and managing everything around it.\n\n"
                "**Common use cases**:\n"
                "- **Payments**: start a payment, refund it in full or in part, and look up what was "
                "charged and what it cost\n"
                "- **Customers**: create and update customers, attach payment sources to them, and credit "
                "or debit their balance\n"
                "- **Billing**: raise invoices and subscribe customers to recurring prices\n"
                "- **Reconciliation**: read the balance and the transactions behind each payout, so a "
                "deposit can be matched against the payments that made it up\n"
                "- **Connect**: onboard connected accounts, add their payout destinations and the people "
                "on their legal entity, and act on their behalf\n\n"
                "Actions are generated from Stripe's own OpenAPI 3 schema, pinned to one API version, so "
                "the fields an action offers and the shape it returns are the ones Stripe documents for "
                "that version.\n\n"
                "Authentication is a Stripe secret or restricted key, or an access token from Stripe "
                "Connect's OAuth2 flow. A **restricted key**, limited to the resources the integration "
                "needs, is the better choice in production.\n\n"
                "Stripe is a trademark of Stripe, Inc.",
            "scheme": "stripe",
            "logo": Priv::StripeLogo,
            "logo_file_name": "stripe-logo.svg",
            "logo_mime_type": MimeTypeSvg,
            "groups": (DataProvider::AppGroup::Payments,),
        });

        # register every action in the manifest
        RestSchemaActionSet action_set = StripeSchema::getActionSet();
        action_set.registerActions(StripeDataProvider::AppName);
    };
}

/** @mainpage StripeDataProvider Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section stripedataproviderintro StripeDataProvider Introduction

    The %StripeDataProvider module provides a @ref dataproviderintro "data provider" API for
    [Stripe](https://stripe.com), the payments platform.

    @section stripedataprovider_generated Generated, Not Hand-Written

    The actions this application exports are **generated from Stripe's own OpenAPI 3 schema** by the
    @ref restschemaactionsintro "RestSchemaActions" module, rather than being hand-written.  A schema
    describes the transport contract precisely and is maintained by Stripe; duplicating several hundred
    request and response fields in %Qore would mean finding and reproducing every upstream change by
    hand.

    What a schema cannot say is which of an operation's fields a user should see first, which
    identifiers should be selectable rather than typed, or that a wire-level integer is really a date.
    Those come from an application-owned manifest and UX overlay in
    @ref StripeDataProvider::StripeManifest "StripeManifest".

    @section stripedataprovider_pinned The Pinned Schema

    The schema is committed to this module rather than fetched at run time, so an upstream change
    cannot alter the contract of an action that existing workflows already call:

    |!Property|!Value
    |Source|https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stripe/openapi/master/openapi/spec3.json
    |SHA-256|\c 3653ad45bbec54fcbe461c541c908355b715018bdf455a0e11b27bedb2cbdee5
    |Stripe API version|\c 2026-07-29.dahlia
    |Imported|2026-08-08

    The committed copy is pruned to the exported operations and everything they reference — 21 of 416
    paths and 1022 of 1440 component schemas.  That is not only a size reduction: parsing the full
    document takes around 45 seconds, which is not a reasonable cost for an application exposing a few
    dozen operations.

    The @ref StripeRestClient::StripeRestClientBase::DefaultApiVersion "Stripe-Version" header sent by
    the @ref striperestclientintro "StripeRestClient" module is pinned to the same release, so what the
    actions declare and what Stripe returns describe the same API.  **The schema and that header must
    be updated together.**

    @section stripedataprovider_actions Actions

    38 operations are exported, in these groups:

    |!Group|!What it covers
    |Account|the account the credentials belong to
    |Balance|funds Stripe holds, and the ledger behind each payout
    |Charges|charges, including the legacy direct-charge API
    |Customers|customers, their payment sources, balance entries and subscriptions
    |Invoices|invoices
    |Payment Intents|starting a payment
    |Refunds|refunding a payment
    |Connected Accounts|Stripe Connect onboarding and management

    Identifier options are dropdowns wherever the application exports an operation that can list the
    values, so a user picks a customer rather than pasting a \c cus_ ID.

    @section stripedataprovider_amounts Amounts and Timestamps

    Two conventions are worth knowing before using these actions:

    - **Amounts are integers in the currency's smallest unit.** \c 2000 is $20.00 in USD, but ¥2000 in
      JPY, which has no minor unit.
    - **Stripe's timestamps are counts of seconds since the epoch.** Every one of them is presented as
      a %Qore @ref date "date" and converted on the way out, so the unit is decided once rather than by
      each caller.

    @section stripedataproviderrelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection stripedataproviderv1_0 StripeDataProvider v1.0
    - the initial version of the %StripeDataProvider module
*/

#! contains all public definitions in the StripeDataProvider module
public namespace StripeDataProvider {
}
